  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Supporting displaced and crisis-affected populations in Cameroon&apos;s Far North, Northwest, and Southwest regions for recovery and resilience buildingthrough the humanitarian-development-peace nexus</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This initiative builds upon IOM&apos;s experience in the Far North, North-West, and South-West (NWSW) regions, having engaged over 70,000 individuals from various sectors and communities in identifying and prioritizing their needs and objectives. The proposed project aims to deploy an integrated approach in areas affected by displacement and instability due to conflicts in these three regions, with the goal of encouraging dialogue, trust, and cooperation among different stakeholders. Through this approach, the initiative will focus on strengthening access to basic services and meaningful participation of displaced communities, with particular emphasis on women, youth, and other vulnerable groups. This includes displacement tracking through the ETT tool, mobile psychosocial support, distribution of RRM kits, improved access to potable water, and hygiene awareness Furthermore, the project is designed to enhance socio-economic empowerment, particularly for displaced and returnee women and girls in Cameroon, acknowledging the heightened adversities and risks they encounter due to both displacement and societal exclusion. To achieve this outcome, the initiative will organize vocational training and mentorship activities, support and strengthen women-led community organizations and associations, and provide specialized assistance for entrepreneurial ventures. The project is committed to integrating a conflict-sensitive, gender-sensitive, and intersectional perspective at all phases of implementation, empowering women and girls, fostering collaboration with relevant stakeholders, and leveraging the vital support and expertise of the Government of Japan (GoJ)</narrative>
  </description>
